WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:09.480
If the car wash workers can organize, any
workers can organize.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:21.780
In 2015 demonstrators including union leaders
and elected officials

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:26.460
blocked traffic and were arrested in front
of a small car wash in Brooklyn.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:27.780
It was an empowering moment.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:31.460
These workers were willing to stand out there
to fight for their rights

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.600
and for basic human dignity.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.300
I mean that's something that you take with
you for the rest of your life.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.660
And you have the ability to make change by
coming together

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:42.460
and when you do that, sometimes you find that
you've got some friends on your side.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.710
In the past six years industries like car
washes that employ low skilled workers

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:50.260
have been the target of lawsuits for alleged
underpayment of wages.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.560
And they both engage in rampant labor law
violations.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:55.650
An ambitious unionization drive.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.060
Let's hear it then!

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:02.900
And a successful campaign to raise the minimum
wage in New York

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:04.340
to 15 dollars per hour.

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:08.760
We're gonna lead the way, the nation's going
to watch us

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.420
and we're gonna raise up this state.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.300
But here's what really happened.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.600
Instead of helping New York's low wage workers

00:01:15.609 --> 00:01:17.520
the movement is destroying their jobs

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.240
it's pushed some car washes to replace their
employees with machines

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:22.880
and others to close down.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:27.280
Increasingly, workers have no choice but to
ply their trade out of illegal vans

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:28.420
parked on the street.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:32.960
Because the minimum wage has made it illegal
for anyone to hire them at the going rate.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:36.720
At the same time, businesses that have chosen
to automate are benefitting.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.400
Thank you so much for $15 dollars an hour,
the best thing that could happen to us.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.020
Because outlawing cheap labor

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:47.580
makes it harder for new competitors to undercut
them on price and service.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.680
This is an in depth look at the real world
consequences

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:55.340
when politicians interfere with a complex
industry that they don't understand

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.499
enabled by media coverage that rarely questions
their simplistic tale

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.900
of exploited workers in need of protection.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.980
Protect against who?

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:25.520
The car wash campaign serves as a model for
what might be possible.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:30.720
In 2012 a coalition of progressive groups
led by the national retail workers union

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:35.280
launched an ambitious campaign to transform
New York City's car wash industry.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.640
The genesis of the campaign came out of the
realization that you had an industry

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:42.760
which was really just a breeding ground for
terrible conditions

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.739
hottest days of the year and the coldest days
of the year

00:02:44.739 --> 00:02:49.010
these workers are outside, working 60-70 hours
a week and sometimes more

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:51.549
and not even getting payed the minimum wage.

00:02:51.549 --> 00:02:53.930
I think workers felt that they didn't have
many choices

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:57.100
and they really had no one that they could
turn to.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:01.520
And we felt that we could have a real impact
on the industry.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:04.000
But the truth is that almost from day one

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.500
nothing about the car wash campaign has gone
as planned.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:12.440
For starters, it turns out that most workers
don't want thelp from organized labor.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:15.690
After 6 years organizers have unionized 11
businesses

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:18.600
or about 4% of the city's car washes.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:22.180
Two of them have since closed down and the
union withdrew at three more

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.870
because of a lack of support from the workers.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.260
There are just 6 unionized shops remaining,

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:30.800
or about 2% of the city's registered car washes.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:43.069
Ervin Par is an immigrant from Guatemala who
works at Main Street Car Wash in Queens

00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:46.300
one of the city's six remaining unionized
shops.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:50.240
Organizers have held two strikes at this location
in the past few years.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.960
And the New York Times has covered allegations
of worker mistreatment here.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:56.540
Now Main Street could become the fourth car
wash

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:58.620
where the workers pressured the union to withdraw.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:09.420
Protect against who?

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.180
At this particular time we represent the workers
there

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:31.040
and we certainly hope that we'll be able to
continue to do that.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.360
With the unionization drive floundering,

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.180
the movement's leaders shifted their focus
to getting the city and state to mandate change.

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:45.220
The most significant government intervention
championed by the union

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:48.480
was an increase in the minimum wage to $15
per hour.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:53.200
New York is the progressive capitol of the
nation.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:57.000
We fight for fairness, we fight for justice.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.420
This move is upending the industry, but not
in the way activists intended.

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:06.480
We've heard over the years from employers
repeatedly

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.760
that any time that we've made changes within
the industry

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:15.200
that either they'd all automate or the industry
would suffer massive shutdowns.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.800
We haven't seen that happen to date.

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:20.320
In reality, that's exactly what's happening.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:25.660
The $15 dollar minimum wage is driving many
New York car washes to replace men with machines.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:30.820
Such as at this large operation in Queens
that installed a new arch for hosing down vehicles

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:32.960
which will replace about 4 workers.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:37.940
Like many of the city's car washes, this one
was designed at a time when manual labor cost less

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:40.600
than installing and maintaining machinery.

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:44.880
Labor was cheap, yeah, real cheap.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:48.200
Amir Malki is a second generation car wash
builder.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.820
When he started in the industry in the 1980s

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:54.560
operators all over the city were actually
dismantling machinery

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:58.980
because rag, hose and brush wielding men did
a better job for less.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:00.900
Today, it's reversing.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:05.140
Labor's expensive and we need the proper equipment
to clean

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:07.860
so we don't have to use extra men.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:09.760
It's not just renovations,

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:12.900
new fully automated car washes are opening
up

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:16.430
such as this state of the art outfit near
JFK airport

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:20.710
with electronic gates, a self serve vacuum
and a single manager on site

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:23.780
making sure everything is running smoothly.

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:28.880
Car wash owners are choosing to automate even
though it entails substantial risk.

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:33.460
Take Best Auto Spa in Brooklyn, one of the
city's premiere handwashes.

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:36.720
Drawing clients who care deeply about the
appearance of their cars

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:39.300
and are willing to pay more for the human
touch.

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:44.280
The $15 minimum wage means that this business
model is simply no longer viable.

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:48.970
So Best Auto Spa is transforming from the
equivalent of an artisanal bistro

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:51.520
to becoming just another fast food joint.

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:55.620
Two years ago the owner installed $2000 worth
of equipment

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:57.980
which allowed him to lay off 8 workers.

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:02.700
Now he's facing another policy change that
would further increase his labor costs.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:06.840
Employers are currently allowed to attribute
a portion of the tips earned by their workers

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:09.220
towards meeting the minimum wage requirement.

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:14.100
New York State is seriously considering a
proposal to eliminate the so called tip credit.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:16.340
If that happens come January,

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:19.150
The owner of Vegas Auto Spa says he'll have
no choice

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:23.280
but to give all these employees the pink slip
and go fully automated.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.620
Then you have MM Wonder Bar, Church Avenue,

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:28.140
you have new fully automated places opening
up

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:32.680
like City Car Wash express on Northern Boulevard
and Staten Island, I could go on.

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:34.420
What is your take on these examples,

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:37.580
that I think do actually show a major trend
towards automation.

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:39.870
I get it if you go into a car wash and the
owner has said

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:41.920
'I've automated because of what's going on
here.'

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:46.080
that it makes you think that it's like, you
know, part of a much broader story.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:47.180
I'm not saying it isn't the case,

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:50.720
I'm justsaying that it's like that might not necessarily be the case.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:55.960
It's true that not every car wash owner is
willing to take on the risk and expense of automation

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:57.540
and there's another option:

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:01.420
exit the business and relinquish their land
for more profitable uses.

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:04.660
Which has also been happening at many New
York car washes.

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:09.080
Several car washes were closed after their
owners were sued for paying off the books

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:11.280
and not complying with labor regulations.

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:14.300
We will continue to keep an eye on these businesses

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:19.380
to ensure that they don't stray from the path
of righteous business practices.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:23.860
A lawsuit contributed to closing of this car
wash in Woodside Queens,

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:27.900
to this one in Harlem and to this one in upper
Manhattan.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:32.020
At each of these locations, dozens of low
wage jobs disappeared.

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:36.860
Are workers really better off when the car
wash that they worked for closes down?

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:38.540
Yeah, I don't agree with your assessment

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:43.000
that these car washes are closing as a result
of wage and hour lawsuits.

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:46.600
That might not necessarily be the case

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:50.020
that may be how it's being characterized to
you.

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:51.760
Another unintended consequence

00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:55.000
is that for business owners who choose to
stick it out and automate

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.710
the $15 minimum wage actually protects them
from competition

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:02.040
by making it harder for new car washes to
open up.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:05.700
Solely from being a businessman the increase
in the minimum wage

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:08.240
makes my business so much easier.

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:12.950
The best thing that could happen to me and
I think to the industry.

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:16.300
Jack Belinsky is the manager of a new car
wash in Queens.

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:19.980
It opened last year at the site of yet another
labor heavy operation

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:23.700
that closed following a wage and hour lawsuit.

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:28.180
The new owner converted it to a fully automated
exterior-only car wash.

00:09:28.180 --> 00:09:32.200
Meaning customers are left to vacuum the interiors
of their own vehicles.

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:37.100
Cause we used to do the same thing with 25
people, now I'm doing it with 2.

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:39.140
By making cheap labor illegal,

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:44.140
the $15 minimum wage made it possible for
Belinsky to downgrade his service.

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:47.360
Before, if I go exterior then my competition
says

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:51.200
'ah, he went exterior and I'm still full service,
I'll take all his customers.'

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:53.800
That never gave me a chance to go exterior,

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:58.220
right now everybody is forced to go exterior
because of this crazy law

00:09:58.220 --> 00:10:00.540
and the minimum wage $15 an hour.

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:04.880
Now everybody, it evened out the field.

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:09.340
The $15 minimum wage amounts to government
prohibition of low wage work.

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:11.440
And yet just making something illegal,

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:14.600
won't stop able bodied men with few alternatives

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:17.560
from meeting a market demand for their services.

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:25.500
These workers have few options and little
power, they live in the shadows.

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:30.520
The irony is that progressives have pushed
car wash workers further into the shadows.

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:33.120
Since legitimate car washes can no longer
hire them

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:35.120
workers are going to the streets

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:39.380
where it's all cash, no taxes, no unions,
no worker's comp, no insurance

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:41.620
and certainly no wage floors.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:52.120
Fausto, who asked to be identified by his
first name only, is an illegal car wash worker.

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:58.600
He's part of a three man operation washing
cars on the curb out of a van for about $15 a pop.

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:10.600
He's lived in the US for 19 years

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:14.520
and still sends a portion of his earnings back to the Dominican Republic

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:16.940
to help support his wife and children.

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:28.800
How the hell can you, how can I compete?

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:32.760
Because of these guys, their paying cash,
you understand?

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:37.820
They're not paying the proper taxes, FICA,
insurance, but I am.

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:43.420
And now the established car washes, left with
no choice but to install expensive machines

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:46.340
and plaster their walls with operating licenses

00:11:46.340 --> 00:11:50.460
are the ones clamoring for the government
to enforce the minimum wage.

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:52.900
'We worry for the workers, look at the rules
that we make.'

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:56.800
But if those rules are not enforced, those
laws are toothless.

00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:01.160
They only destroy the good guy that tries
to follow all the rules

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:04.360
and has to compete with the bad guy that breaks
all the rules.

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:14.220
Some of these guys working out of these vans
don't have other employment options

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:18.780
are you sure that it would be in their best
interest to shut them down?

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:22.400
Yeah I actually, I do and I'm going to be
very blunt about that.

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:27.640
You can always make the argument that you
can allow some people to skirt the law

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:31.320
some people to skirt regulations that are
meant to protect workers

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:34.960
in an effort to give people work opportunities.

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:37.240
That's a devil's bargain.

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:42.520
Or maybe the real devil's bargain is championing
a set of policies that sound good at a rally

00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:47.840
but that in the real world jeopardize the
livelihood of the working poor.

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:50.320
Which brings us back to Vegas Auto Spa,

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:55.860
the Brooklyn car wash that progressive activists
made an example of back in 2015.

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:03.520
Shortly after the car wash unionized, the
owner started planning his exit strategy.

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:08.280
Two years later he found a buyer who kept
it running for one more year.

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:10.620
Today Vegas Auto Spa is shuttered

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:15.660
and the ripple effects of the entire movement
have been to destabilize an industry.

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:19.800
Pushing the men and women who worked in it
even deeper into the shadows.

